it's another day and night of protests  over the killing of George Floyd  remained mostly peaceful thousands  marched in major cities across America  from Boston to Memphis to Los Angeles  there were some arrests mostly for  violating curfew hundreds of protestors  blocked a bridge in Portland Oregon last  night laying down with their hands  behind their backs Anthony in in Floyd's  hometown of Houston a protester and a  police commander hugged and took a knee  recognizing each other as equals  tens of thousands showed up for a march  in Floyd's honor protests in Washington  DC lasted into the night with some  people throwing fireworks and water  bottles at troops and other law  enforcement officers working with police  they responded with pepper spray from  behind a newly constructed fence that  enlarges the White House security  perimeter  US Representative Ayanna Presley is  calling for all levels of government to  adopt new policies and reforms the  Massachusetts Democrat introduced a  resolution in the house to condemn  police brutality and racial profiling  and she joins us now from Boston  congresswoman good morning it's been  very interesting in the aftermath of  George Floyd's death to see people on  both sides of the political aisle say  they condemned the killing but then  they're not very specific about what  needs to happen next  you are being specific so what is your  vision for police reform well first let  me be clear and say the reason why I  think it's important that a  congressional resolution is brought to  the floor and voted on is because  congressional intent is a powerful tool  and we are in the Democratic majority we  have the most diverse and representative  Congress in the history of Congress and  we should act like it and in this moment  Congress must function as the conscience  for our nation and that is why I've  introduced a resolution in partnership  with our the chair of the Congressional  Black Caucus Karen bass and  representative Omar denouncing police  brutality racial profiling and excessive  force we use congressional resolutions  to set a value sprayin when it comes to  foreign policy the last time  Lucian like this was introduced was in  1999 by representative Danny Davis it  never made it to the floor and I cannot  begin to approximate the number of black  lives that have been lynched brutalized  murdered surveyed profiled from 1999 to  now there is unrest everywhere and it  will persist for as long as there is  unrest and so this congressional  resolution at this tipping point moment  is an important affirmation and then we  have many bills in the queue that the  Congressional Black Caucus will be  rolling out that get us to those broader  reforms do you think congresswoman that  there is agreement on this idea of a  tipping point it's been interesting to  hear the president's national security  advisors say there is not racism in  policing in this country I talked to the  chief of the NYPD here in New York City  he does not believe there is a systemic  problem is that just a difference in  vocabulary or a fundamental difference  in vision it's a fundamental difference  in many things and I really don't have  much patience for care what Donald Trump  and or anyone a part of his corrupt  administration who codifies their  cruelty and their bigotry in policy  every day depending on the moment Donald  Trump has been either a hapless  bystander or an insider of violence  someone who refers to my community as  the blacks is not anyone who I am  listening to at this moment he is  completely calloused disconnected and  removed from the hurt and the trauma the  generational hurt and trauma as a black  woman married to a black man raising a  black child I would very much like to  pass on to my 11 year old daughter  something other than generational trauma  and to be clear the unrest that is  happening the peaceful protesting and  demonstrating is not only about police  brutality bear in mind the black  community is disproportionately bore the  brunt of koban 19 we are managing that  trauma and that loss a pandemic within a  pandemic a public health pandemic and  the scurbs that is police brutality  layered with the true  of housing injustice economic injustice  education injustice and healthcare  injustice James Baldwin said not  everything that his face can be changed  but nothing can be changed until it is  faced and as a nation we must face our  failure in this moment we have  legislated hate and hurt it is  disproportionately been put on black  folks and we must now legislate healing  and justice yeah well when we talk about  healing it tends to imply a some sort of  a coming together when you think about  the nation as a whole how do we come  together when it seems there are so many  different stories about what America is  right now there's a story that you're  telling and then there are the voters  for the president for many of your  opponents on the other side of the aisle  in Congress and they don't recognize the  America that you're describing so how do  you bridge that divide because the only  way you're gonna get anything passed of  course is if everybody votes as a  majority but that's why what is  happening the organizing and to be clear  community organizers the people that I  was with in community and our black  lives matter local protest last night  community organizers are community  builders not destroyers the only thing  we seek to destroy or dismantle is  structural racism and systemic  oppression and so public pressure is  important it holds us accountable the  reason why we have often acted with  conviction as lawmakers is because of  the courage of everyday people the civil  rights movement we're still in it it's  not over but early freedom writers like  John Lewis who I have the honor of  serving with they gave us the blueprint  organized mobilized legislate now we'll  pretend that it's hard to be encouraged  given this gop-led Senate which just  yesterday was voting on lifetime  appointments another Trump  lifetime appointment instead of bringing  real relief to the American people in  the midst of the pandemic and passing  the heroes Act instead of acknowledging  the scourge that is police brutality and  racism but Congress this is  Gustav body and institution one that  I've been connected with for 20-plus  years I'm new to Congress as a member  but I've been associated with this body  for decades as an intern as an aide and  I have tremendous respect for this body  and my colleagues we have denounced evil  many times before and we should do it  now  so congresswoman given that we're in a  situation nationally where we're roughly  half the population probably doesn't  agree with you could be more than half  the population how hopeful are you that  change will come in the wake of George  Floyd's killing and be lasting change  comes when we organize for it when we  mobilize for it and when we demand it  and you know so far as you're wondering  how many people agree with me when I'm  in community we were with thousands of  people yesterday from every walk of life  and I just want to take a moment to  acknowledge the youth leadership that we  continue to see on the front lines of  this movement this movement will not be  co-opted we will be sustained we will be  vigilant and we will be precise in our  demands because the legislation that has  been put upon black folks codifying hurt  and trauma for generations was very  precise and very targeted so in this  moment we too must be precise we must be  focused and so I am very hopeful all  right we can end on a hopeful note then  at least for a moment anyway a  representative Presley I appreciate your  time this morning and hope you have  thank you much  thank you very much congresswoman

